Date: Sun, 28 Mar 93 22:53:33 PST
From: hkhenson@cup.portal.com
Subject: Traffic guide

Organizing Traffic with Computers



                                                                         
by H. Keith Henson

I can safely state that computers will eventually drive vehicles for us.  

When that time comes, they should be able to do a much better job of it 
than we do.  It is also safe to say that this isn't going to happen for 
some number of decades.  The task of driving a vehicle is much more 
complicated than it first appears, and may require a computer approaching 
the computational capacity of a human brain to do it well.  

The most powerful computers in the world today are in the sub milli-brain 
range [footnote 1, Moravec], so they must improve by at least a factor of 
1000 in capacity, and another factor of 1000 in cost before we can expect 
vehicles to come equipped with electron chauffeurs.  While this sounds 
radical, there are no technical limits to computers' improving a million 
to one--again.  The cost of computing has already dropped by a factor of 
a million to one over the past few decades. 

[footnote 2:  Dr. Stanley Schmidt once asked an advanced physics class if 
it would be possible to pack the computational power of a human brain 
into such and such a space (handwaving a 10 inch cube).  After getting 
general agreement that this was very unlikely to impossible, he pointed 
out that there were (presumably) several examples in the room.] 

However, we face problems with parts of our greatly overloaded road 
system NOW.  An interesting question is what might be done with the 
relatively primitive computers of today to help drivers drive.  
Considerable work has already gone into computer activated anti-lock 
brakes and internal computer monitoring and controlling of the mechanical 
state of a vehicle, but such developments have had near-zero effects on 
normal traffic flow.  "Driver assistant" concepts have concentrated on 
communicating alternative routes to drivers, not on improving their 
performance in dense traffic.  Those involving computer screens have 
recognized safety problems distracting the drivers. 

Another serious consideration is the inclusion or retrofit problem if any 
part of a system must be placed in the vehicle.  The average vehicle is 
on the roads about 14 years.  The tail of the age distribution dates back 
to the very beginning of modern automobiles. 

With these restrictions in mind, a system to assist drivers should 
(ideally) have the following characteristics: 

1.  Be useable by drivers in unmodified vehicles. 

2.  Non- or minimally distracting to the driver (heads up). 
        
3.  Produce significant increases in traffic flow. 

4.  Have a reasonable cost, i.e., a system must cost a good deal less for 
a given increase in traffic throughput than just pouring more concrete. 

Considering the last point first, the cost of pouring concrete is at 
least a million dollars a lane-mile even where right of way does not have 
to be purchased, or interchanges constructed.  Completely new freeways in 
urban areas are several times that expensive.  So, a significant increase 
in traffic capacity (say 10%) which costs less than $100,000 a lane-mile 
would be a good tradeoff.  

A hundred thousand dollars will not buy enough computer to drive a car, 
but it will buy a fair amount of computer nowadays.  Recent _retail_ 
prices for Intel 486 computers with a megabyte of memory have been under 
$1000.  Memory cost alone of a million dollars a megabyte are well within 
my own professional experience.  On the very low end, four-function 
calculators cost $3-4 each.  In fact, you could replace lane striping 
paint with four-function calculators for an incremental cost of about 
$50,000 a lane mile.  It wouldn't take much of an improvement in traffic 
capacity to make expenditures in this cost range well worthwhile. 

The most successful uses to date of control systems to improve traffic 
flow are traffic metering onto freeways and timed signal lights.  Neither 
requires modification of the vehicles, and both are highly effective and 
well-accepted driver signaling devices.  

Low-cost computers and displays permit an extension of the concepts of 
timed traffic lights to an effectively continuous traffic control signal 
specific to each vehicle.  While such a system can be adapted to major 
surface streets, it is less complex to apply it to a freeway. 

One physical implementation of a continuous traffic-control signal would 
consist of light-emitting diodes (LEDs) spaced about two inches apart, 
encased in polycarbonate plastic and glued to the road surface near the 
center of each lane--perhaps offset to be in line with left side driver 
seats.  Vehicle location could be determined by IR transmitter/receiver 
pairs in the strip located at intervals of a few feet. 
                                                        
Red/green/amber LEDs could be used to instruct drivers in an obvious 
slowdown/speedup/hold speed & spacing, but such a display lacks spacial 
information required for that segment of the population with color vision 
problems. 

A purely spacial method would be to illuminate a segment of the LED strip 
from just in front of a vehicle to a few feet behind the proceeding 
vehicle with an alternating light/dark pattern.  To be used as a 
vehicle-specific control signal, the illuminated segment must propagate 
at the same speed as the vehicle.  Instructions to drivers to adjust 
their speed/spacing would be superimposed on the light/dark pattern by a 
second order "motion" within the pattern. 

The best way I have found to try to explain such a second-order motion is 
to imagine a letter "O" going around the Times Square display.  If the 
"O" were to have an internal line added, it would look like a clock.  An 
observer moving in sync with the display would see the clock hand 
"moving" forward or backwards as the "clock face character" were slowly 
modified on its trip around the building.  Applied to a one-dimensional 
LED strip, drivers would see a motion within the illuminated segment in 
front of their vehicles, in spite of the segment's being propagated at 
the same speed as the vehicle.  Even if it is difficult to explain this 
phenomenon, programming it into a microprocessor is a trivial exercise.  

One intuitive selection of signal would be for the second-order motion to 
look as if it were crawling away from the driver as an indication that 
the vehicle should speed up.  Crawling backwards toward the driver would 
be the signal to slow down, and a "stationary" pattern would indicate 
that the vehicle was exactly where it should be in the moving traffic 
stream.  The signals were picked to be similar to hand signals used to 
direct traffic. 

Drivers would be instructed to speed up or slow down as indicated by the 
pattern motion.  As a vehicle got closer to the desired speed and 
spacing, the second-order pattern motion within the illuminated segment 
would slow to a stop.  Driving in sync to such a pattern should be no 
more difficult (and produce no more driver stress) than keeping pace with 
another vehicle. 
                         
In addition to the LEDs for driver signals and the active IR vehicle- 
detection sensors mentioned above, the strips would contain support 
circuits, power buses, optical fibers for communication, and 
microprocessors every 200 feet or so.  To detect vehicles the IR sensors 
will have to use a mix of strategies such as shadows in sunlight and 
reflected IR at night.  A vehicle would be detected by several sensors a 
second, so failure of some of the sensors would not be a serious matter.  
Likewise, drivers would be seeing hundreds of LEDs per second, so failure 
of a small fraction of the LEDs would not seriously degrade the signal to 
drivers. 

The strips themselves would have to last some number of years to be 
economical.  Reflector dots are made of polycarbonate plastic, so at 
least the plastic and glue is known to survive on roadways.  It will take 
good engineering and considerable testing to make connections to the 
strips for power and signal which survive being run over by heavy trucks.  
The strips will have to be recessed in areas with snow so they are not 
scraped off by snow plows.  Methods will have to be devised to quickly 
replace sections damaged by dragging trailers and wrecks. 

Data on vehicle location and time at that location would flow to non-stop 
roadside computers rated at a million instructions per second (MIPS) or 
higher.  Rough analysis of the problems indicates that a one-MIPS 
computer could manage about one lane-mile of traffic.  Data on where to 
place the illuminated segments, and what pattern to display to drivers 
would flow back to the LED strip.  The microprocessors in the strip would 
concentrate location data and manage display propagation and driver 
signals.  This would greatly reduce the communication rate, and offload 
the roadside computers so they could concentrate on higher-level traffic 
management.   

Without considering details of any control strategy, it is easy to 
predict two problems--motorcycles and drivers who just ignore the 
signals.  Motorcycles could be equipped with IR beacons if they wanted to 
be included in the traffic optimization, or they could be ignored.  
Drivers who ignore the system by trying to go too fast do not appear to 
be very much of a control problem if the system is turned on only during 
conditions of high to very high traffic density (i.e., commute traffic).  
A tailgating driver would appear to the control system as an extra long 
vehicle.  A more difficult problem would be caused by drivers who insist 
on driving slower than the "ambient" traffic no mater how slow the 
traffic is moving.  A slow driver might be treated as a special case of a 
blocked lane, and the traffic flow managed accordingly. 

If most drivers obey active speed and spacing instructions (and most obey 
metering lights and similar signals), then a number of potential traffic 
flow managements schemes can be considered.  One would be to organize the 
traffic into platoons broken at intervals by gaps permitting lane 
changes, and then move the gaps so that drivers would be offered frequent 
lane-change opportunities.  By always removing cars from the following 
platoon and adding them to the leading platoon, the traffic "holes" would 
propagate backwards relative to the traffic stream, permitting cross-lane 
movement.  There is a slight similarity here to the semiconductor concept 
of "conduction by holes."  Incidentally, lane-switching would be an 
ineffective tactic for drivers in a hurry since each lane change would 
move them backwards relative to the vehicles around them. 

Maintaining holes at proper spacing throughout the traffic stream could 
be accomplished by splitting and combining platoons.  This would be a 
dynamic process orchestrated by a network of roadside computers.  The 
roadside computers would be in constant communication with similar 
adjacent lane, upstream and downstream computers.  The computers would be 
passing data on the vehicles to be expected downstream, and passing data 
on the traffic conditions, emergencies and the like upstream.  As usual 
in such systems, 95 percent of the programming effort will be for 
exception conditions.  Controlled merging from ramps into prepared holes 
seems to be a conceptually manageable process, especially since metering 
is already an accepted practice.  I expect that the most acceptable and 
efficient traffic control methods will be found after a few installations 
have been made, and the programmers have a better understanding of both 
the limitations and the impressive abilities of drivers. 

Early reviewers were concerned about liability problems, and possibly the 
need for special legislation.  But since this does not seem to cause 
problems with metering lights or traffic lights, liability may be a 
manageable concern. 

Of course, driver acceptance of a control system is a critical issue.  
However, I think that most drivers, especially those who commute, would 
accept a system which fairly imposed order on chaotic (in the technical 
sense) traffic if it kept the traffic moving smoothly. 

Once installed, there are two optional modifications drivers could 
install on their vehicles.  The IR sensors provide a limited 
communication channel as well as sensing the vehicles.  A module less 
complex than a TV remote control could be connected to the turn signals.  
It would transmit a driver's merge intent to the strip under his vehicle.  
The computer system controlling traffic would be designed to accept such 
requests and respond by opening a hole in the lane next to the vehicle 
making the request.  An additional signal, such as blinking the entire 
segment in front of the vehicle could be used to signal the driver that 
the requested merge was now possible. 

The IR transmitters in the strip could also be used to send a signal to 
vehicles duplicating the speedup/slowdown data presented in the LED 
pattern in front of a vehicle.  This information could be used to adjust 
the cruise controls in vehicles equipped to receive it.  Neither of these 
modifications would be necessary, but both would be convenience items, 
especially for commute drivers. 

A follow-on (and considerably more difficult) project would be to apply a 
similar control system to major streets.  A side pointing "ok to enter 
from an uncontrolled street or driveway" signal would need to be added to 
the strips.  Perhaps green equally spaced LEDs, and red unequally spaced 
LEDs would do for a signal.  Traffic would be bunched into platoons, and 
the speed of the platoons dynamically varied over a fairly wide range so 
as to permit cross traffic platoons (also under control) to be well out 
of the way as vehicles went through intersections.  Shades of those 
wonderful old silent era films, where four vehicles just miss each other 
going through an intersection!  On a serious note, a high fraction of 
urban air pollution comes from idling vehicles.  Hitting "all lights 
green" would nearly eliminate this source, as well as greatly improving 
urban traffic flow.  

In this case, there is an answer to the age old question "if this is a 
good idea, why hasn't already been done."  It might _not_ be a workable 
concept, but until computers had fallen from multi-million dollar 
monsters to their current prices these concepts were simply out of the 
question.  

Even if current computers make the idea thinkable, development will 
require a wide-ranging multidisciplinery team from parts of the technical 
community who rarely talk to each other.  I would like to hear 
criticisms, concerns, and suggestions from readers and reviewers (even if 
this article is not published). 



