Richard Kennaway, presumably replying to my comment about publishers not
reading unsolicited manuscripts (a point expanded on by others as well):

>I've never heard the plagiarism-avoidance reason before.  Some publishers
>do read unsolicited manuscripts.  The problem is that the slush-pile, as it
>is known, consists almost entirely of worthless junk.  Remember Sturgeon's
>Law -- and he was only talking about the stuff that gets into print!  Some
>publishers may well decide it isn't worth their while to bother.  Those who
>do read the slush-pile develop a fine art of assessing a story with a
>single glance at the first paragraph.  The vast majority of the slush-pile
>is rejected on that basis alone.  It's rather like reading high-volume
>mailing lists.

The same applies to corporations receiving letters from inventors
describing their nifty new inventions: most corporations return the
material unread, fearing that an similar projects they may have in
development or ready to market will be "tainted" (not a precise legal term,
I know) by the unsolicited idea.

I had thought the business of publishers (especially scripts for movies)
not looking at "over the transom" manuscripts was fairly well known, but
apparently not. This is another reason for agents: the agent ensures that
all the right releases have been signed, thus allowing the publisher to
look at the material with less fear that he'll be sued by an opportunist.
(Obviously, disputes still occur, as in the Art Buchwald case against Eddie
Murphy's "Coming to America" movie.)

It is likely that in a polycentric law world, things would move back toward
what they used to be: an unsolicited manuscript could be looked at and
then, if a mutually advantageous,  a deal struck, with essentially little
chance for a successful lawsuit based on nebulous ideas of "influence."

And crypto techniques also change the equations significantly.

